      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Acute lacunar stroke in subjects under 55 years of age has been poorly characterized."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We assessed the clinical features of lacunar stroke in 51 patients aged ≤55 years (84.5% men, mean standard deviation [SD] age 49.8 [5.2] years) collected from a prospective hospital-based stroke registry in Barcelona, Catalonia, Spain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "This subset of young lacunar stroke patients accounted for only 5.2% of all lacunar strokes, 1.2% of all ischemic strokes, and 1.1% of all acute strokes included in the registry over a 24-year period. In the multivariate analysis, factors independently associated with acute lacunar stroke in patients aged ≤55 years were alcohol consumption (>60 g/day) (odds ratio [OR] = 6.67), heavy smoking (>20 cigarettes/day) (OR = 3.02), obesity (OR = 2.81), essential etiology (OR = 2.73), and headache at stroke onset (OR = 2.45)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Characterization of the clinical profile of acute lacunar stroke in younger patients contributes to a better knowledge of the full clinical expression of this ischemic stroke subtype."
        }
      ]},
